const projectSummaries={"aether":"An interactive GPU particle field with 65,536 particles.","buenos-aires-refuges":"Find climate shelters and drinking fountains across Buenos Aires.","matcha": "An acquired app that turns handwritten notes into study tools.", "marketplace": "A student marketplace for life across international rotations.", "evoluer": "A fragrance concept that evolves with the seasons.", "bulby": "A desktop meeting copilot with live transcription and screenshot context.", "beacon": "A tsunami preparedness app for the Kamaishi City Government.", "cyber": "Teaching command-line skills through capture-the-flag challenges.", "vectorizer": "Turn images and videos into grayscale matrices.", "nfa": "Explore DFA, NFA, and PDA diagrams through interactive computation trees.", "preclass": "Collect Forum readings or workbook questions for a ChatGPT study session.", "mymizu": "A refill-map prototype for building sustainable hydration habits.", "green-carbon": "A field-mapping and farmer-logbook prototype.", "yuki-issei": "A music, website, and branding project with UTAITE.", "instagram": "Compare Instagram followers and following locally.", "setlist": "Track workouts and export your gym log.", "homework-hound": "A pixel dog that makes it look like your homework was eaten."};
const expandedDescriptions={"buenos-aires-refuges":"Buenos Aires Climate Refuges is a bilingual React and TypeScript prototype for finding climate shelters and drinking fountains. It searches a local dataset of 269 shelters and 176 fountains with filters and record details. An optional Google Maps layer adds map selection and walking directions; the core search works without an API key. The app has no backend or account system.","aura-protection":"Aura Protection is a concept for making attention boundaries visible instead of burying them in settings. The interaction centers on a small, playful pause before a distracting action. The interesting product challenge is designing an interruption that feels helpful without becoming another distraction.","beacon":"Beacon was an academic tsunami preparedness project for the Kamaishi City Government. We worked with the city’s tsunami chips, which already support a similar purpose, while exploring how a digital interface could guide someone through an emergency. The app organizes hazard information into a short sequence of actions so a person can find the next step under time pressure. The design prioritizes location, alert state, and offline access while keeping instructions easy to scan.","bffr":"BFFR is a digital reality-check concept with a deliberately direct voice. It turns a moment of overthinking into a short prompt to reconsider an assumption. The design experiment is how much personality an interface can have while still giving people a clear, useful interaction.","bulby":"Bulby is an Electron desktop overlay that transcribes microphone and call audio locally with faster-whisper. I connected the live transcript and a fresh screenshot to a Chrome extension that hands context to ChatGPT only when the user asks. The compact window can expand, move, and resize; the local transcription path needs no OpenAI API key.","caughtcha":"Caughtcha is a small JavaScript experiment about noticing online habits in the moment. It uses a well-timed interruption as the central interaction rather than a dashboard of retrospective statistics. The interesting design constraint is making the prompt noticeable without making it disruptive.","christmas-lights":"Christmas Lights is an interactive seasonal screen experiment built in JavaScript. Instead of a static holiday image, it treats light and motion as the interface. The project is a small study in timing, visual feedback, and making a browser feel like an object in a room.","cyber":"Cyber for Students taught command-line and networking fundamentals across 16 countries. I provisioned dedicated Linux virtual machines so learners could practice in their own environments. The curriculum used 250 custom capture-the-flag challenges, turning shell and security concepts into problems students could solve hands-on.","delusiongpt":"DelusionGPT is a TypeScript command-line companion for stressed developers. It uses Commander and Inquirer for the terminal flow and Chalk for its intentionally theatrical responses. The interesting constraint is expressing a distinct personality through text, prompts, and timing instead of a graphical interface.","evoluer":"ÉVOLUER is a L’Oréal Brandstorm fragrance concept built around one base scent that changes through the seasons. We designed a spray-count mechanism for releasing monthly scent capsules and mapped the experience from bottle use to an in-store Fragrance Observatory. The work combined product mechanics, scent-journey prototyping, and brand design; it won the Japan national round in 2026.","green-carbon":"Green Carbon is a working field-mapping and farmer-logbook prototype. It uses Leaflet for polygon drawing and overlap checks, Supabase for profile and field data, and local storage as a fallback when the service is unavailable. The workflow links boundary geometry to activity records so farm verification is easier to inspect, including in low-connectivity settings.","homework-hound":"Homework Hound is a Chrome extension that sends a pixel dog across the current page to “eat” the homework. JavaScript controls the dog’s appetite, the page effect, stopping, and cleanup so the interaction is reversible. It can also export an evidence.png screenshot of the joke.","instagram":"Instagram Follow Checker compares followers and following from Instagram exports entirely in the browser. It reads multiple ZIPs or extracted JSON/HTML, combines numbered follower files, and deduplicates usernames before showing searchable results. The ZIP parser handles stored and deflated entries, including ZIP64, without an account, server upload, or installation.","lockin-twin":"Lockin Twin is a focus-companion app built with React and TypeScript. Its repository uses MediaPipe Face Mesh for camera-based face tracking and Framer Motion for interface animation. The technical experiment is whether a responsive character can make the start of a work session feel less solitary.","matcha":"Matcha turned handwritten notes into AI flashcards and practice tests for active recall. As CPO, I led product decisions, branding, and growth while shaping the note-to-study workflow. Matcha was acquired in June 2026 for integration into a productivity-app suite.","marketplace":"Minerva Marketplace is a peer-to-peer marketplace designed for a student community of about 1,000 across international rotations; that is the target community, not a claimed active-user count. I designed PostgreSQL schemas for users and listings, implemented OAuth, and set up scheduled email notifications. The interesting systems problem is keeping listings and transactions useful as students change cities each semester.","mymizu":"Mymizu is a React prototype for making water refills easier to find and repeat. The consumer side combines a Google Maps refill map, daily refill tracking, and a growing sprout companion; an admin view diagnoses underserved “red zones” and suggests new stations. I explored how geospatial analysis, reminders, and small rewards can connect individual behavior to network coverage.","nfa":"Automata Lab is a JavaScript workbench for editing DFA, NFA, and PDA diagrams and inspecting their computation trees. It supports step-by-step exploration, explicit epsilon transitions, and PNG, SVG, or clipboard export.","preclass":"Preclass is a Manifest V3 Chrome and Edge extension for Minerva Forum. It collects readings or numbered workbook questions into a reviewable prompt and sends it to a chosen ChatGPT course conversation. The workbook collector checks for missing questions; the handoff preserves existing drafts and offers a copy fallback. It runs without a backend, API key, analytics, or inference billing.","seen":"seen. is a TypeScript and Next.js experiment built around the feeling of being left on read. Its repository includes React, an OpenRouter integration, and scripts for iMessage interaction. The interesting technical question is how to turn a familiar messaging signal into a responsive experience rather than a static joke.","setlist":"Setlist is a weekly gym dashboard that stores workout entries in the browser. It records start and end times, tracks exercises by day, and attaches up to eight photos per session. Weekly duration and session totals are calculated automatically, and the displayed week can be exported as CSV.","speakeasy-terminal":"Speakeasy Terminal is a concept for a hidden space navigated through typed commands. It treats command parsing, discoverability, and feedback as the experience rather than hiding them behind conventional menus. The design challenge is making a terminal feel inviting to someone who has never used one.","vectorizer":"Vectorizer converts images and video frames into 50 × 50 grayscale matrices for a linear-algebra exploration. A Python CLI can export CSV, JSON, or Sage-compatible matrix text, while a Flask app processes uploaded images and clips. I varied epsilon thresholds and replayed processed frames to inspect information loss and motion continuity.","yuki-issei":"Yuki Issei combined a musician’s web presence, visual identity, and short-form content. I built the site, edited clips and a lyric video, designed the poster, and coordinated the production workflow. AI-assisted song drafts became a starting point for human vocal recordings with the singer."};
